From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 14:54:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 90/91] fix(ci): absorb an edge rotation between checksum and
 download (gcp-kinb)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The Supported Pack Nightly's bmad job went red on 2026-09-10 with a Gas
City checksum mismatch at `Install Gas City` — and neither the expected
nor the actual checksum in that log was still what the `edge` release
published by the time anybody read it. Nothing was corrupt: the
installer resolves the expected checksum in one request and downloads
the archive in a second, and `edge` is a rolling pre-release whose asset
goreleaser replaces in place several times a day. A job whose download
straddles a rebuild verifies the bytes of build N+1 against the digest
of build N. Only a cache-missing job downloads at all, so which of the
six parallel pack jobs loses the race is a function of shared tool-cache
state rather than of the pack — reading it as "bmad is broken" sends the
next reader into the wrong pack entirely.

A mismatch is now diagnosed instead of trusted. On a mismatch the
checksum is re-resolved: unchanged means the bytes really are wrong and
that still fails loudly, with the diagnosis spelled out so the next
reader is not left guessing which of the two it was. Moved means the
asset rotated under us, and the download is accepted when it matches
what the release publishes *now* — the same server-side digest check,
just against build N+1 — or retried against the new checksum otherwise.
Retries are bounded at three so a genuinely broken asset, or an `edge`
rotating faster than a download completes, fails rather than spinning.
The cache path became a function because of that: an entry has to be
keyed on the checksum actually verified, never on whichever one was
resolved first, or a rotation would file build N+1 under build N's key
and reintroduce the staleness that key exists to prevent.

Verified by running the script for real against a scripted stand-in for
the release — a `curl` on PATH serving a release that rotates exactly
when a test says it does, no network. The three new rotation and
corruption tests fail against the pre-fix script, the rotation one with
the nightly's own signature ("Gas City checksum mismatch for
edge/<platform>", exit 1); the undisturbed install and the warm-cache
reuse pass either way and are the controls. A real mismatch keeps
exiting 1 after exactly one download, installing nothing and exporting
no GC_BIN.

Upstream `edge` rotation is deliberate and out of scope here: the fix is
local to this fork's installer.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
